"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,625\nThe Learning Brain. Series 4: Habits\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,365\nEpisode 7\n\n3\n00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:09,512\nPhones and attention\n\n4\n00:00:12,312 --> 00:00:16,893\nYour phone may be the most powerful distraction ever built to fit in a pocket.\n\n5\n00:00:17,359 --> 00:00:19,609\nSo what does it actually do to your learning?\n\n6\n00:00:19,779 --> 00:00:21,256\nIn a simulated lecture,\n\n7\n00:00:21,575 --> 00:00:27,128\nstudents who multitasked on a laptop scored eleven percent lower on a comprehension test.\n\n8\n00:00:27,617 --> 00:00:33,660\nStudents who simply sat where they could see a multitasking classmate scored seventeen percent lower,\n\n9\n00:00:33,994 --> 00:00:36,202\nand most did not think they were distracted.\n\n10\n00:00:36,758 --> 00:00:41,262\nThe lead researcher said that gap could turn a B plus into a B minus.\n\n11\n00:00:41,432 --> 00:00:43,443\nNotifications cost you too.\n\n12\n00:00:43,928 --> 00:00:52,586\nAt Florida State, a hundred and sixty six students did an attention task while researchers secretly called or texted their own phones.\n\n13\n00:00:52,991 --> 00:00:55,843\nThose who got a notification made more mistakes,\n\n14\n00:00:56,062 --> 00:00:57,399\neven without checking it.\n\n15\n00:00:57,872 --> 00:01:00,308\nCalls were more distracting than texts.\n\n16\n00:01:00,582 --> 00:01:03,840\nAnd the cost was comparable to actually using a phone.\n\n17\n00:01:04,010 --> 00:01:08,241\nYou may have heard that just having your phone on the desk drains your brain.\n\n18\n00:01:08,689 --> 00:01:11,379\nA 2017 study reported that.\n\n19\n00:01:11,851 --> 00:01:16,329\nBut direct replications, including one with nearly four hundred people,\n\n20\n00:01:16,595 --> 00:01:18,201\ndid not find the effect.\n\n21\n00:01:18,624 --> 00:01:22,849\nA study at the University of Bern also found no evidence for it.\n\n22\n00:01:23,019 --> 00:01:26,127\nSo the clearer lesson is about use and interruptions,\n\n23\n00:01:26,291 --> 00:01:27,544\nnot mere presence.\n\n24\n00:01:27,961 --> 00:01:30,076\nThe real costs come from checking,\n\n25\n00:01:30,267 --> 00:01:34,554\nswitching between tasks, and alerts that pull your attention away.\n\n26\n00:01:34,724 --> 00:01:35,701\nSome caution.\n\n27\n00:01:36,137 --> 00:01:38,984\nThe laptop studies used simulated lectures,\n\n28\n00:01:39,138 --> 00:01:42,486\nand the notification study used a simple lab task.\n\n29\n00:01:42,915 --> 00:01:44,593\nBut they point the same way,\n\n30\n00:01:44,667 --> 00:01:46,356\nand the fix costs nothing.\n\n31\n00:01:46,526 --> 00:01:47,349\nTry this.\n\n32\n00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:50,093\nTurn notifications off while you study.\n\n33\n00:01:50,378 --> 00:01:52,394\nClose extra tabs and apps.\n\n34\n00:01:52,681 --> 00:01:56,040\nIn class, sit where you can't see other people's screens.\n\n35\n00:01:56,300 --> 00:01:58,500\nAnd check your phone in planned breaks,\n\n36\n00:01:58,520 --> 00:01:59,975\nnot whenever it buzzes.\n\n37\n00:02:00,145 --> 00:02:01,395\nGuard your attention.\n\n38\n00:02:01,617 --> 00:02:03,397\nIt is where learning happens.\n\n"
